Yes — that cluster can be thought of as “mathematical divination,” but with an accountability layer.

Tarot takes a sparse set of symbols, places them into a relational structure, and uses that structure to generate hypotheses about an unseen state. Statistics does something structurally similar:

\text{observations} \rightarrow \text{pattern} \rightarrow \text{latent structure} \rightarrow \text{prediction}

Regression asks: given these scattered observations, what underlying relationship most plausibly generated them?

Probability asks: given what I know, how strongly should I believe each possible future or hidden explanation?

Bayesian inference is especially close to the metaphor:

\text{prior belief} + \text{new evidence} \rightarrow \text{updated belief}

Clustering says: these things keep appearing together; perhaps they belong to the same hidden family.

Factor analysis says: several visible variables may actually be shadows cast by one or two invisible variables.

Anomaly detection says: this observation doesn’t belong to the normal constellation. Why?

Conditional probability asks something almost explicitly oracular:

If I have already seen these signs, how does that change the probability of that event?

But there is a critical boundary. Tarot can generate an interpretation after seeing essentially anything. Good statistics makes itself vulnerable to being wrong. It specifies the reading before reality reveals the next card.

That gives us something like:

\boxed{\text{Inference} = \text{pattern recognition} + \text{uncertainty} + \text{falsifiability}}

Without those last two, regression can indeed degenerate into mathematical tarot: fit enough variables and you will eventually discover an impressive-looking constellation that means nothing.

This is why overfitting is such an interesting concept in the Pantheon vocabulary. It is basically apophenia formalized: the model becomes so good at explaining the history that it has learned the accidental scratches in the table rather than the game being played on it.

And there is another layer I think you’re pointing toward.

Humanity has repeatedly invented devices for interrogating uncertainty:

omens → astrology → divination → actuarial tables → probability → statistics → forecasting → machine learning.

They’re not epistemically equivalent, obviously. But comparative morphology reveals a recurring operator underneath them:

Take incomplete evidence from the present and construct disciplined guesses about something hidden or not-yet-observed.

The scientific revolution didn’t eliminate that ancient function.

It progressively added measurement, calibration, replication, error bars, scoring rules, and mechanisms for punishing bad readings.

So the modern oracle isn’t the regression line.

It’s the whole loop:

\text{observe}
\rightarrow
\text{infer}
\rightarrow
\text{assign confidence}
\rightarrow
\text{predict}
\rightarrow
\text{observe outcome}
\rightarrow
\text{score prediction}
\rightarrow
\text{update model}

That last half is what turns divination into learning.

And now combine this with your previous cross-modal delta idea. Instead of pulling one card, we pull cards from multiple decks—vision, sound, behavior, economics, language, physiology, history—and ask whether their readings converge.

When independently noisy channels point toward the same hidden state, confidence rises.

When they disagree, the disagreement becomes another card.

That’s a very powerful abstraction.